# Chroma library, modern Earth: TITLE MOMENTS of faith and causes (titles volume 2), an everyday pair per title that only its holder meets (holds:), and JOINING pairs open to anyone, where some acts take a new community or faith title (title:).
# Syntax: header of build.py. Rules: library-spec.md. Brief: drafts/titles2/MOMENTS-BRIEF.md. Seeds: Emren's "Modern Earth: 100 More Titles" (each gated pair's first moment grows from the title's turning point).

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- member of a monastic community (T2-080): calls RG (cycle s1) / calls WUB (cycle s2)

== a hard companion in the quiet life
tier: everyday
rate: 0.5
stages: young_adult adult mature elder
age: 20-90
calls: RG
tone: trouble
life: faith, home
horizon: months
roles: a fellow member, elder
holds: member of a monastic community
earth: the quiet life of the community still holds washing up, disagreement, and one member whose habits are hard to bear
times: Nearly every member of a religious community, most years; bearing with a hard companion is an old theme of the monastic rules (estimate)
gap: 0.8-1
scene: The quiet life still has its washing up. Every evening {N} dries the plates beside the same member of the community, who hums the same four notes, bangs the cupboard doors and corrects how everyone else stacks the cups.
scene W: The rule asks each member to bear the others' weaknesses with patience. {N} has said those words at morning prayer for three years, and tonight they sound like a test.
scene U: {N} has started counting: eleven corrections tonight, nine of them about cups. Something lies underneath it, and {N} would like to know what.
scene B: {N} gave up a salary, a flat and a career in the city to live like this, by choice. {N} did not give all that up to be told how to stack cups.
scene R: The cupboard door bangs for the fourth time, and {N} grips the tea towel so hard the knuckles ache.
scene G: The house has stood for two hundred years and outlived every quarrel inside it. The cups will still be stacked wrongly when both of them lie in the garden cemetery.
- W | keep silence at the sink, and offer the irritation up at evening prayer | habit | v: tradition | self_control: + | chance: 92
- U | ask them quietly what the four notes are, and really listen to the answer | v: benevolence | chance: 75
- B | remind yourself that you chose this life, and let no one person spoil it | v: self-direction | self_control: + | chance: 90
- R | tell them straight out, once, that the constant corrections are too much | v: none clear (honesty of feeling) | chance: 55
- G | let their ways be part of the house, like the draughts and the bells | v: tradition (humility) | chance: 85
- W>U | name their faults at the community's meeting, so the head of the house corrects them | v: conformity | mark: made an enemy | closed: approval: accusing a fellow member in front of the house; backfire: the house closes ranks around them | chance: 40
- U>B | keep a private note of every correction, in case it ever comes to a complaint | habit | v: power | closed: approval: keeping a file on a fellow member; backfire: the note is found and the house takes sides | chance: 85
- B>R | offer to take their turn at the pots if they leave your evenings in peace | v: self-direction, hedonism | chance: 75
- R>G | ask them out on a walk round the fields after evening prayer, and talk | v: benevolence | chance: 65
- G>W | ask the oldest member how the house has always borne such people, and do likewise | v: tradition, conformity | chance: 80
works: By winter the evenings at the sink are quiet, and once or twice the two of them laugh.
works: The irritation settles into the rest of the house's weather, and {N} sleeps well again.
fails: The cupboard doors bang on, and {N} dreads the evening meal.
fails: Word of the quarrel reaches the head of the house, and the whole community takes sides over cups.

== the community's guesthouse needs a manager
tier: everyday
rate: 0.5
stages: young_adult adult mature elder
age: 20-90
calls: WUB
tone: mixed
life: faith, home
horizon: years
roles: elder, a fellow member
holds: member of a monastic community
earth: the head of the community asks you to take over its guesthouse, which is losing money and guests
times: Many members of religious communities, once or twice in a life; houses hand out duties like the guesthouse every several years (estimate)
gap: 6-12
scene: At the community's meeting the head of the house reads out the guesthouse accounts, worse than last year, and then asks {N} to take it over in the spring.
scene W: Obedience was the first promise {N} made here. The head of the house has asked, and in this house that question usually has one answer.
scene U: {N} has already been through the figures twice: empty rooms midweek, a website nobody updates, and a heating bill that eats everything else.
scene B: The guesthouse brings in the only money that comes from outside, and whoever runs it deals with the bank, the council and the press.
scene R: Booking forms and boilers, when {N} came here to pray. Part of {N} wants to laugh, and part of {N} wants to run.
scene G: The guesthouse has taken in travellers since before the railway came, and the visitors' book on the hall table holds a hundred and forty years of names.
- W | say yes, as your promise of obedience asks, and start in the spring | binds | v: conformity, tradition | mark: kept your word | chance: 85
- U | go through the accounts line by line, and bring a plan to the next meeting | v: achievement | chance: 80
- B | accept, and run it like a business, with your own targets for the year | v: achievement, power | chance: 65
- R | admit you are no longer sure this life is right, and ask to leave | identity | v: self-direction | mark: left home | drops: member of a monastic community | self_control: - | chance: 80
- G | ask to stay with the garden and the bees, where you know you belong | v: tradition, security | mark: stayed home | chance: 45
- W>B | run it strictly by the book, and make the house independent of outside money | v: security, power | chance: 60
- U>R | plan music weekends and walking retreats to bring the old place back to life | v: stimulation | chance: 40
- B>G | sell the old annexe to a developer, so the main house stays the community's | v: security, tradition | mark: made an enemy | closed: approval: selling part of what the founders left; backfire: the older members never forgive it | chance: 45
- R>W | throw yourself in, and paint every guest room before the first visitors of the year | body: light | v: benevolence | chance: 78
- G>U | spend a season watching who comes and why, before changing anything | v: self-direction | chance: 65
works: By summer the rooms are full on weekdays, and the boiler is paid for.
works: The community accepts {Ns} answer, and the matter is settled without a quarrel.
fails: The guesthouse loses more money than ever, and the community's meetings grow tense.
fails: {Ns} answer leaves the head of the house disappointed, and the silence at meals feels heavier.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- congregation musician (T2-082): calls WUG (cycle s3) / calls BR (cycle s4)

== the old harvest song in a hard year
tier: everyday
at: faith body
rate: 0.5
stages: young_adult adult mature elder
age: 16-90
calls: WUG
tone: mixed
life: faith, music
horizon: week
roles: parent, elder, the worship committee
holds: congregation musician
earth: the song that always closes the harvest service sings of full barns, in the year the town's factory closed
times: Some congregation musicians, once or twice in a career; harvest comes every year, but a town's hard year far less often (estimate)
gap: 6-12
scene: For forty years the harvest service has closed with the same song about full barns and plenty. This spring the factory shut, and {N}, who chooses the music, sits at the keyboard with the order of service half written.
scene W: The song is printed in the order of service every year, and the oldest members sing it without looking at the words. Changing it is not {Ns} choice alone.
scene U: {N} plays the song through alone in the empty building, listening to the words: barns, plenty, hands that never want. Perhaps another verse, or another key.
scene B: The congregation will remember who handled this well. {N} would like it to be the musician, not the committee.
scene R: Singing about plenty to people who lost their jobs in the spring: the thought makes {Ns} throat tighten.
scene G: {parent} sang this song at every harvest, and so did the grandparents. Songs like this have carried the town through hard years before.
- W | take the question to the worship committee, and play whatever they decide | v: conformity | chance: 85
- U | keep the tune everyone knows, and write new words for this year | identity | v: benevolence, self-direction | chance: 55
- B | choose the music yourself, since choosing it is what the role is for | v: self-direction, power | chance: 75
- R | play the old song slowly, as a lament, and let the room feel the year | v: benevolence | chance: 80
- G | keep the old song as it is: it has carried the town through worse | identity | v: tradition | chance: 70
- W>R | play the song as printed, then end with a fierce, joyful piece of your own | v: tradition, stimulation | chance: 72
- U>G | find the old verse about lean years that newer songbooks dropped, and restore it | v: tradition | chance: 75
- B>W | get the minister on side before the committee meets, so the matter is settled | v: power, security | chance: 65
- R>U | improvise something new on the day, and see what the room makes of it | v: stimulation, self-direction | chance: 45
- G>B | keep the old song, and let it be known the founding families would walk out | v: power, tradition | mark: made an enemy | closed: approval: using the old families as a threat; backfire: the newer members stop coming | chance: 35
works: The last chord rings out, and several people in the pews are wiping their eyes.
works: After the service the oldest member and the youngest both stop by the keyboard to thank {N}.
fails: Half the congregation sings one thing and half another, and the service ends in a muddle.
fails: A letter of complaint about the music reaches the office before the week is out.

== a gifted newcomer at the keyboard
tier: everyday
at: faith body
rate: 0.5
stages: young_adult adult mature elder
age: 16-90
calls: BR
tone: trouble
life: faith, music
horizon: months
roles: rival, elder
holds: congregation musician
earth: a gifted newcomer plays better than you, and the congregation has started asking for them
times: Many congregation musicians, once or twice in a career; a stronger player moving to town is a rare turn (estimate)
gap: 6-12
scene: {rival}, a student who moved to town in the autumn, played at the carol service while {N} was ill. Since then three people have asked when {rival} is playing again.
scene W: {N} has played every week for nine years, through snow and two funerals in one week. The role was given by the congregation, and it is not a contest.
scene U: {Ns} trained ear catches it at once: {rival} finds chords {N} has never thought to use, the kind that take years of study.
scene B: {N} has the place on the rota, the keys to the building and a small fee every month, and has no intention of losing any of them.
scene R: {rival} plays with a grin, and the whole room sways. {N} feels a hot sting of jealousy and, under it, the urge to play like that too.
scene G: Every musician here was once the newcomer, {N} remembers, and every one of them was replaced in time.
- W | offer the newcomer two services a month, and draw up a fair rota | v: universalism, benevolence | chance: 75
- U | ask the newcomer to teach those chords, and practise them every evening | habit | v: achievement | mark: learned a skill | self_control: + | chance: 90
- B | remind the council, before anyone asks, that the post and its fee are not open | v: power, security | chance: 85
- R | ask the newcomer to play a duet at the next service, and give the room everything | v: stimulation | mark: made a friend | chance: 85
- G | hand the role on to the newcomer, the way your own teacher once did | v: benevolence, tradition | drops: congregation musician | chance: 80
- W>G | insist the newcomer serves a year on probation, as the old rules require | v: conformity, tradition | closed: approval: blocking a newcomer with procedure; backfire: the congregation hears why and takes the newcomer's side | chance: 45
- U>W | write out arrangements for two players, so every service sounds fuller | v: benevolence | chance: 60
- B>U | take the newcomer under your wing, learn all they know, and keep the main services | door | v: achievement, power | mark: learned a skill | chance: 50
- R>B | play the hardest piece you know next week, so nobody forgets who the musician is | identity | v: power, achievement | chance: 65
- G>R | stop fretting, and enjoy hearing someone else play from the pews for once | v: hedonism | chance: 90
works: By spring the music is fuller than it has been in years, and nobody is counting who plays when.
works: {N} plays at the next feast, and the room sways the way it did for {rival}.
fails: The congregation splits into those who want {N} and those who want {rival}.
fails: {rival} stops coming altogether, and people blame {N}.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- civil-liberties campaigner (T2-086): calls WR (cycle s1) / calls UBG (cycle s2)

== defending a speaker you cannot stand
tier: everyday
rate: 0.5
stages: young_adult adult mature elder
age: 18-85
calls: WR
tone: trouble
life: cause, community
horizon: months
roles: colleague, the street preacher, the campaign's lawyer
holds: civil-liberties campaigner
earth: the campaign is asked to defend a street preacher banned from the market square, whose views you find hateful
times: Most civil-liberties campaigners, every few years; defending speech they hate is a classic test of the cause (the ACLU and Skokie, 1977)
gap: 3-6
scene: A street preacher whose views {N} finds hateful has been banned from the market square under a vague new bylaw, and the campaign's lawyer wants {N} to speak for his right at the hearing.
scene W: The bylaw lets an officer ban anyone whose speech "causes annoyance". Today it is the preacher; the rule does not care who comes next.
scene U: {N} reads the bylaw, then the preacher's leaflets, then the bylaw again. The legal question and the personal one are not the same question.
scene B: The hearing will be in the papers, and whoever argues it well becomes the name people call next time.
scene R: {N} has heard what he shouts in the square, and standing beside him turns {Ns} stomach. A ban on speech makes {N} angrier still.
scene G: The market square has heard preachers, hawkers and drunks for six hundred years, and the town has always found a way to live with its loudmouths.
- W | speak for his right at the hearing, and say plainly that you reject his views | identity | v: universalism | chance: 70
- U | write a careful brief on why the bylaw is too vague to stand | v: universalism, achievement | chance: 70
- B | take the case on for the attention it will bring the campaign | v: power | chance: 85
- R | refuse to stand beside him, and say why at the campaign meeting | v: self-direction | chance: 92
- G | let others argue this one, and keep your strength for causes closer to home | v: security | chance: 90
- W>U | hold the campaign to its rule: every case judged on the law, not the person | v: universalism | chance: 90
- U>B | pass a journalist the council emails you were copied into by mistake | v: power | mark: made an enemy | closed: law: using documents sent in error; backfire: a complaint, and the campaign's name in the story | chance: 45
- B>R | take the case only if you never have to share a platform with him | v: self-direction | chance: 75
- R>G | go to the square and talk with him face to face, one person to another | door | v: benevolence, universalism | chance: 55
- G>W | propose the town's old answer: a set corner and a set hour for street speakers | v: tradition, security | chance: 20
works: The bylaw is suspended, and nobody mistakes {Ns} defence of the right for agreement with the man.
works: {N} comes out of the week able to look everyone in the eye, and the campaign holds together.
fails: A photo of {N} beside the preacher goes round the town, and old friends stop calling.
fails: The hearing upholds the ban, and the campaign argues about the case for months.

== a seat on the council's camera panel
tier: everyday
at: council
rate: 0.5
stages: young_adult adult mature elder
age: 18-85
calls: UBG
tone: mixed
life: cause, community
horizon: months
roles: colleague, a councillor, grandparent
holds: civil-liberties campaigner
earth: the council offers the campaign a seat on its panel on new face-recognition cameras, if the court challenge is dropped
times: Some campaigners, once or twice in a life; councils often offer a seat at the table in place of a fight (estimate)
gap: 6-12
scene: The council wants face-recognition cameras in the town centre. It offers the campaign a seat on its advisory panel, on one condition: the court challenge goes away.
scene W: A legal challenge is the proper route, and the campaign promised its supporters it would see the case through to the end.
scene U: {N} reads the panel's terms of reference: advisory only, meetings in private, minutes approved by the council. The trap is in the wording.
scene B: A seat at the table means early drafts and the ear of the people who decide. The court case could take three years, and might lose.
scene R: {N} feels the old fury rise at the idea of being bought off with a chair and a lanyard.
scene G: The cameras will stand on those lamp posts for twenty years or more. {N} thinks about the streets {Ns} grandchildren will walk.
- W | refuse the seat, as the campaign promised its supporters it would fight in court | identity | v: conformity, universalism | mark: kept your word | self_control: + | chance: 75
- U | go through the panel's terms line by line, and propose new wording first | v: self-direction, achievement | chance: 50
- B | take the seat, and shape the cameras from the inside | v: power | mark: broke your word | chance: 60
- R | stand up in the council meeting and say exactly what the offer is | identity | v: self-direction | mark: defied an authority | chance: 85
- G | hand the campaign to younger members, and give your evenings back to home | v: benevolence, security | drops: civil-liberties campaigner | self_control: - | chance: 75
- W>B | accept, but only in writing, with public minutes, and hold them to it | v: power, security | chance: 20
- U>R | map every camera site and publish the map, so people can choose their routes | identity | v: self-direction, universalism | mark: defied an authority | chance: 90
- B>G | trade the seat for cameras kept out of the park and the side streets | v: security | chance: 35
- R>W | lead a sit-in at the council chamber until the offer is withdrawn | body: light | v: universalism | closed: law: occupying a council building; backfire: removed by the police and charged | chance: 20
- G>U | wait and watch how the cameras work in the next town before choosing a side | door | v: security, self-direction | chance: 70
works: The council backs down on the worst of it, and the campaign keeps its supporters.
works: The choice holds up over the year, and the campaign comes out of it stronger than it went in.
fails: The cameras go up anyway, and the campaign splits over who sold out whom.
fails: The council quietly drops the offer, and the court case drags on with no money left.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- digital-rights advocate (T2-088): calls UB (cycle s3) / calls WRG (cycle s4)

== the free app the volunteers want
tier: everyday
at: charity
rate: 0.5
stages: young_adult adult mature elder
age: 18-80
calls: UB
tone: mixed
life: cause, community
horizon: months
roles: colleague, the food bank's coordinator
holds: digital-rights advocate
earth: the food bank's volunteers want a free app that would make their work easier, and nobody knows who would keep its records
times: Most digital-rights advocates, every year or two; small charities often take up free tools from big companies (estimate)
gap: 2-4
scene: The food bank's volunteers are tired of the shared spreadsheet and want the free app a big company offers charities. They ask {N}, and nobody can say who would keep the names and addresses of the families they help.
scene W: There are rules about keeping records on people who come for help, and someone has to answer for them. In this group that someone tends to be {N}.
scene U: {N} reads the terms of service at the kitchen table: forty pages, and the word "partners" on almost every one.
scene B: Whoever picks the system decides what the group can see and do. {N} would rather that was someone who understands it.
scene R: The volunteers are worn out, and the old spreadsheet crashes every Friday. {N} feels the pull to say yes and get on with feeding people.
scene G: The group has run on paper lists, then a spreadsheet, for twenty years. Whatever it uses next has to outlast the people who set it up.
- W | warn the coordinator you will report the group to the regulator if it signs up | v: conformity, security | mark: made an enemy | closed: approval: threatening your own volunteers with the regulator; backfire: the group goes ahead and drops you from the rota | chance: 40
- U | read all forty pages, and tell the group plainly what happens to the data | v: universalism, self-direction | chance: 85
- B | build the group a simple system of your own, so you control who sees what | binds | v: power, self-direction | mark: learned a skill | chance: 50
- R | say yes to the app, and get on with feeding people | v: benevolence | chance: 85
- G | keep the old spreadsheet a while longer, and patch it as you always have | habit | v: tradition, security | chance: 60
- W>R | write a short data policy, then let the volunteers pick any tool that meets it | v: self-direction, security | chance: 85
- U>G | find an open tool run by a charity that will outlast any one volunteer | v: universalism, security | chance: 55
- B>W | make yourself the group's data officer, so every records decision goes through you | v: power, security | chance: 75
- R>U | try the app for one weekend with made-up names, and see what breaks | v: stimulation, self-direction | chance: 80
- G>B | keep your own paper copy of the families' list, as the old volunteers always did | v: security, power | closed: law: holding people's details outside the rules; backfire: a complaint to the data regulator | chance: 90
works: The volunteers get their easier Fridays, and the families' details stay where they should.
works: Six months on, the group's records are tidier than they have ever been, and nobody worries about who holds them.
fails: A stranger writes asking what the group holds about them, and nobody knows how to answer.
fails: The argument over the app splits the volunteers, and two of the best leave.

== the town website's keeper wants out
tier: everyday
rate: 0.5
stages: young_adult adult mature elder
age: 18-80
calls: WRG
tone: mixed
life: cause, community
horizon: months
roles: elder, colleague
holds: digital-rights advocate
earth: the retired volunteer who has kept the town's community website and photo archive running for fifteen years is giving it up
times: Some digital-rights advocates, once or twice in a life; many community websites rest on one volunteer (estimate)
gap: 8-15
scene: For fifteen years {elder} has kept the town's community website running alone: the noticeboard, the bus times, the photo archive of every flood and fair. The hosting runs out next month, and the email lands in {Ns} inbox.
scene W: Thousands of people use that site every week, and none of them know who pays for it. It needs a committee and a bank account, not one tired volunteer.
scene U: {N} logs in and finds the code: old, patched by hand, held together by one person's memory.
scene B: The site has more local readers than the newspaper. Whoever takes it over will have the town's ear.
scene R: Fifteen years of photos could vanish with one unpaid bill. {N} is half out of the chair already.
scene G: The site is like the old noticeboard outside the post office: nobody owns it and everybody needs it. Things like that last only while someone tends them.
- W | set up a small trust with a committee, so the site never hangs on one person | v: security, universalism | chance: 70
- U | move the archive onto modern, documented software over the winter | v: achievement | mark: learned a skill | chance: 60
- B | take the site over yourself, and keep the final say on what goes on it | v: power | chance: 90
- R | start a fundraiser that night, and tell the town what it is about to lose | v: universalism, stimulation | chance: 65
- G | sit with the old keeper each week, and learn how every piece of it works | v: tradition, benevolence | mark: learned a skill | self_control: + | chance: 75
- W>G | ask the library to take the archive into its public collection for good | v: tradition, universalism | chance: 75
- U>W | write clear instructions, so any later volunteer could keep it running | v: benevolence | chance: 75
- B>U | sell local advertising on the site to pay a developer to rebuild it properly | v: achievement | chance: 55
- R>B | copy the whole site that night, and relaunch it under your own name | v: power, stimulation | closed: law: republishing other people's photos without permission; backfire: takedown notices and an angry old keeper | chance: 40
- G>R | throw a party in the town hall for the old keeper and the fifteen years | v: benevolence, hedonism | chance: 50
works: The site stays up, the photos stay online, and the old keeper sends a two-line email of thanks.
works: By spring the town's website has new hands behind it and a plan for the next fifteen years.
fails: The hosting lapses for a week, and half the photo archive is lost in the move.
fails: The site survives, but the town quarrels over who runs it now.

