# ---------------------------------------------------------------- choir member (T2-059) / amateur band member (T2-073): calls WG (cycle s1) / calls UBR (cycle s2)

== an open rehearsal at the community choir
tier: everyday
rate: 0.0008
stages: juvenile young_adult adult mature elder
age: 14-85
calls: WG
tone: joy
life: community, music
horizon: months
roles: friend, the conductor
earth: the community choir holds an open rehearsal where anyone may come and sing, no audition needed
times: Many adults, a few times in a life; about 1 in 6 Americans sing in a choir (Chorus America 2019)
gap: 6-12
scene: A poster at the library says the community choir is holding an open rehearsal on Tuesday: no audition, tea afterwards. {N} goes along, and is handed a folder of music at the door.
scene W: The choir has sung at every town winter concert for fifty years. The folder comes with a rehearsal calendar and the dates of the year's concerts.
scene U: {N} cannot quite read music, but by the second piece the shapes on the page are starting to make sense.
scene B: The conductor mentions a solo in the spring concert, still uncast. {N} has sung alone in the car for years and wonders what this room would make of it.
scene R: Forty voices hit the first chord together, and the hair on {Ns} arms stands up.
scene G: {friend} has sung alto here for twenty years, and pats the empty chair alongside.
- W | sign up for the term, and come to every rehearsal | habit | v: conformity, benevolence | title: choir member | requires: singing | without: approval | lacking: .8 | chance: 92
- U | sit in the back row and listen, to work out how the parts fit | v: self-direction | chance: 95
- B | join, and put your name down straight away for the spring solo | identity | v: achievement | title: choir member | requires: singing | without: approval | lacking: .4 | chance: 50
- R | sing your heart out tonight, and leave without signing anything | v: hedonism | chance: 95
- G | take the empty chair beside your friend in the altos, and stay | v: benevolence, tradition | title: choir member | requires: singing | without: approval | lacking: .8 | chance: 82
- W>U | ask for the recordings of each part, and decide once you can sing one piece | v: self-direction | chance: 85
- U>B | ask about the city's chamber choir instead, where the singers are stronger | door | v: achievement | chance: 20
- B>R | book private singing lessons instead, and sing what you like | v: self-direction, hedonism | chance: 92
- R>G | talk a neighbour into coming along next week, and decide together then | v: benevolence, hedonism | chance: 55
- G>W | join, because your family has sung in the town choir for three generations | binds | v: tradition | title: choir member | requires: singing | without: approval | lacking: .8 | chance: 80
works: By the spring concert {N} knows every note of {Ns} part and every face in the room.
works: {N} walks home humming, sure of the choice.
fails: {N} spends the evening a beat behind everyone else, and leaves feeling foolish.
fails: The choir's hall is closed for repairs the next month, and the plan comes to nothing.

== a band that needs a drummer
tier: everyday
rate: 0.0008
stages: juvenile young_adult adult mature elder
age: 14-85
calls: UBR
tone: joy
life: community, music
horizon: months
roles: friend, parent, the band
earth: a card in the music shop: local band seeks drummer, rehearsals on Thursdays in a garage
times: Uncommon: perhaps 1 in 10 adults who once played an instrument meet such a call and weigh it, a few times in a life (estimate)
gap: 6-12
scene: A card pinned up in the music shop says: band seeks drummer, covers and a few songs of our own, Thursdays in a garage on the edge of town. {N} played drums at school and has barely touched a kit since.
scene W: The card asks for someone reliable who will come every Thursday, and {N} knows that is half the job.
scene U: {N} finds the band online: three videos, good songs, and a drummer problem anyone can hear.
scene B: The band plays the pubs around town, and the singer apparently knows someone who books the summer festival. A seat behind the kit could open doors.
scene R: {N} drums the shop's music on the counter without noticing, and the old itch is back.
scene G: {Ns} old drum kit is still under a sheet in the loft at {parent}'s house, where it has waited since school.
